A FOUNDER'S PERSPECTIVE SaaS Isn't Dead. It's Finding Its True North. On the death-of-SaaS narrative, what the investor community is actually reacting to, and why domain expertise is the only moat that holds. AUTHORED BY Brandon M. Weaver brandonmweaver.com brandonmweaver.com

BRANDON M. WEAVER   SAAS ISN'T DEAD. IT'S FINDING ITS TRUE NORTH. brandonmweaver.com   1 The investor community has a narrative right now. It is worth understanding precisely, because getting it wrong in either direction, dismissing it or surrendering to it, costs you. Brandon M. Weaver brandonmweaver.com I have been fundraising for a SaaS company in a market where the word SaaS has become, in certain rooms, almost a liability. A Stanford GSB-trained investor I know, someone with the pattern recognition that comes from backing companies like Facebook at the early stage, told me plainly that the investor community is running away from SaaS. He was not being unkind. He was being accurate. That conversation has been a useful forcing function. Because when a credible person in a credible position tells you the market has decided something, you have two choices: accept it as final, or understand it well enough to know where it is right and where it is incomplete. I have spent time doing the latter. What follows is my honest read of what is actually happening in the SaaS market, why the fear is legitimate, and why it points toward a specific kind of company rather than the extinction of a model. The short version: SaaS is not dying. It is being forced to earn its right to exist. The companies that make that case convincingly, through domain expertise, genuine problem-solving, and an obsessive feedback loop with their customers, will come through this moment stronger than they entered it.

"SaaS is not dying. It is being forced to earn its right to exist." BRANDON M. WEAVER

BRANDON M. WEAVER   SAAS ISN'T DEAD. IT'S FINDING ITS TRUE NORTH. brandonmweaver.com   3 I Acknowledging the Fear: Where Investors Are Right The pressure on SaaS is structural, not cyclical. This distinction matters because structural pressure does not resolve when sentiment improves or rates come down. It requires a different response. AI has materially lowered the cost of building software. Features that once required months of engineering effort can now be prototyped in days. Entire product categories that commanded premium subscriptions because of their technical complexity are being commoditised faster than any previous cycle. If your SaaS company is differentiated primarily by its feature set, that differentiation is eroding in real time. The multiple compression in public SaaS markets reflects this reality. Investors who built mental models around rule-of-40 metrics and net revenue retention are now asking a more fundamental question: what does this company know, or do, that AI cannot replicate cheaply? For a significant portion of the SaaS market, that question does not have a satisfying answer. There is also a customer behaviour shift underway. Buyers who once accepted the friction of enterprise software procurement are increasingly willing to trial AI-native alternatives that solve narrower problems faster and at lower cost. The tolerance for bloated, underused software suites is declining. The bar for genuine value delivery is rising. All of this is real. The investor community is not manufacturing panic. It is responding rationally to observable market signals. The question is whether those signals condemn SaaS as a category, or only a subset of it.